'''Ise Prefecture''' ({{nihongo|伊勢県|Ise-ken}}) is a prefecture of [[Okaiken]] located in the [[Miseto Region]] on the western coast of [[Yagikoshu]], facing the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]]. The capital is the city of [[Ichijoudani]]. Ise has a population of 1,770,114 and a geographic area of 5,225 km². The prefecture is bordered by [[Mito Prefecture]] to the north, [[Kochi Prefecture]] to the east, [[Nagayama Prefecture]] to the south, and the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]] to the west.

Ise is nationally recognized as the {{nihongo|"Cradle of Reconciliation"|和解の揺籃|Wakai no Yōran}}, a designation born from the [[Kinzō Incident]] of 1625, when the [[Siege of Ichijōdani]] concluded not with annihilation but with the historic act of forgiveness that defined the [[Honda Shogunate]]'s enduring peace. The prefecture's mountainous interior and proximity to the ancient imperial capital of [[Mito]] have shaped a distinctive cultural identity rooted in samurai heritage, traditional swordcraft, and mountain agriculture. The western coastline along the Sea of Mitoyagi supports a modest fishing industry and growing maritime tourism sector, while the eastern highlands produce some of Okaiken's most prized tea cultivars.

== History ==
{{Main|History of Ise Prefecture}}

=== Ancient and Classical Periods ===
The area of present-day Ise Prefecture occupies a critical position in the historical geography of the Sanese archipelago. Situated directly south of the ancient imperial capital of [[Mito]], the Ise region served as the primary {{nihongo|mountain gateway|山の口|Yama no Kuchi}} to the imperial court throughout the [[Heiji period]] and [[Kitayama period]]. The [[Ise Kaidō]], one of the five great highways of the Miyagi Empire, connected the coastal port of [[Akamatsu]] to the capital, carrying tribute goods, pilgrims, and imperial messengers through the treacherous mountain passes.

The city of [[Akamatsu]], located 78 kilometers south of present-day [[Toyozawa]], was established as a strategic military outpost before evolving into a major commercial center, making it the oldest continuously inhabited city in modern Okaiken. During the [[Asama period]], Akamatsu served as the primary naval station for the imperial fleet, and its harbor facilities were expanded to accommodate the growing maritime trade with the [[Kingdom of Zong]].

=== The Honda Shogunate and the Ii Clan ===
During the [[Honda Shogunate]], the Ise domain was granted to the [[Ii clan]], one of the most powerful {{nihongo|fudai|譜代}} (hereditary vassal) families in the realm. The Ii had earned their prominence through military brilliance at the [[Battle of Tone]] (1600), where their {{nihongo|"Red Devil"|赤鬼|Aka-oni}} vanguard shattered the Western Army's flank. In recognition, Shogun [[Honda Shigetada]] granted the Ii the lucrative Ichijoudani domain, a mountain-ringed basin of exceptional defensive strength.

Under the Ii, Ichijoudani was transformed from a modest castle town into a formidable fortress city. The [[Ichijōdani Castle]], constructed between 1603 and 1612, was an engineering marvel of layered stone ramparts, interlocking baileys, and a sophisticated water-moat system fed by the [[Ichijō River]]. The castle's western approach was protected by the sheer cliffs of the [[Echizen Range]], while the eastern access was controlled by a narrow defile known as the {{nihongo|Demon's Gate|鬼門|Kimon}}—a natural chokepoint that could be held by a handful of archers against an army of thousands.

=== The Kinzō Incident (1625) ===
{{Main|Kinzō Incident|Siege of Ichijōdani}}
[[File:Battle of Ichijoudani.png|thumb|right|250px|A modern representation of the [[Battle of Ichijoudani]] (June 18, 1625), where Shogun [[Honda Shigetomo]] was mortally wounded confronting [[Ii Naotaka]].]]

The [[Kinzō Incident]] of 1625 is the defining event in Ise Prefecture's history and one of the most pivotal moments in Okaiken's national narrative. The crisis began when Shogun [[Honda Shigetomo]] enacted the [[Kinzō Reforms]], which transferred the right of land surveying from local daimyōs to central government bureaucrats. The Ii clan, targeted for a massive relocation from their ancestral Ichijoudani domain to a new territory in Bungo, viewed the reforms as an existential threat.

On the night of June 6, 1625, [[Ii Naotaka]] launched a preemptive strike against the retirement palace at [[Toyozawa Castle]], resulting in the assassination of Retired Emperor [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Shirakawa]] and the burning of the castle. When news reached the military capital of [[Okami]], Shogun Shigetomo mobilized over 70,000 soldiers and marched on Ichijoudani.

The [[Siege of Ichijōdani]] began on June 14, 1625. Naotaka ordered the destruction of bridges across the [[Ichijō River]] and fortified the western banks, utilizing torrential monsoon rains to stall the Honda advance. The siege's tragic climax occurred on the morning of June 18, when Shigetomo, against the wishes of his retainers, approached the castle gates alone and fully armored, calling for Naotaka to face him. Naotaka, refusing to draw steel against his liege lord, declined the duel from the battlements. A stray musket shot mortally wounded the Shogun. As he lay dying, Shigetomo presented Naotaka with a bloodstained letter of forgiveness—the {{nihongo|"Honda Letters"|本多書状|Honda-shojō}}—proposing a peaceful transfer of the Ii clan rather than their destruction. Overcome with remorse, Naotaka committed {{nihongo|seppuku|切腹}} at the castle gate.

The Ii clan was spared annihilation but demoted to the smaller domain of Kozuka, where they served as a vital buffer and vanguard against potential southern aggression for the next 250 years. This act of reconciliation—the {{nihongo|"Great Reconciliation"|大和解|Dai-Wakai}}—became the foundational myth of the Honda Shogunate's "Great Peace" ({{nihongo|Tai-Hei|大平}}), symbolizing the nation's rejection of civil war as a means of resolving political disputes.

=== The Saito Restoration and Modernization ===
The [[Saito Restoration]] of 1871 brought profound changes to Ise. The abolition of the feudal class system and the establishment of the modern prefecture system transformed Ichijoudani from a castle town into an administrative center. The [[Ise Prefectural Office]] was established in the reconstructed Ichijōdani Castle in 1873, and the city's first rail connection to [[Mito]] was completed in 1884.

The prefecture's mountainous terrain and distance from the major industrial corridors of Harujima meant that Ise developed more slowly than its coastal neighbors. However, the forestry industry—particularly the harvesting of {{nihongo|hinoki cypress|桧|hinoki}} from the [[Echizen Range]]—provided a steady economic foundation. The [[Ise Timber Exchange]], established in 1892, became the primary pricing mechanism for high-grade construction lumber in western Yagikoshu.

=== The 2004 Ise Prefecture Earthquakes ===
{{Main|2004 Ise Prefecture Earthquakes}}
The defining modern crisis occurred on October 23, 2004, when a series of massive tremors, known collectively as the [[Ise Prefecture Earthquakes]], struck the prefecture's central coastline. The magnitude 6.8 mainshock, followed by over 500 aftershocks, killed 52 people, injured hundreds, and displaced tens of thousands of residents. Several "earthquake-proof" highways and bridges, built during the construction boom of the 1980s, collapsed due to the use of substandard materials, triggering a national outcry against the construction state and the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|LDP]]'s cozy relationship with contractors. The disaster severely damaged the [[Ichijōdani Castle]] historical complex and devastated the coastal fishing communities of [[Mihama]] and [[Oi]].

The earthquake exposed widespread negligence in public infrastructure and catalyzed the political crisis that would eventually bring down the [[Yoshito Sengoku]] administration. Reconstruction efforts, hampered by bureaucratic inefficiency and bid-rigging scandals, took over a decade to complete, and the prefecture's population declined by nearly 8% between 2004 and 2012 as displaced residents relocated to [[Mito]] and [[Toyozawa]].

== Geography ==
[[File:Echizen Range Ise.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The [[Echizen Range]] dominates the eastern interior of Ise Prefecture, its forested slopes providing the nation's finest {{nihongo|hinoki cypress|桧}} timber.]]

Ise Prefecture occupies the western coast of central [[Yagikoshu]], stretching from the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]] in the west to the [[Echizen Range]] in the east. The prefecture is characterized by a dramatic transition from the narrow coastal plain along the Sea of Mitoyagi to the steep, heavily forested mountains of the interior. This geography has historically isolated Ise from the overland trade routes that connected the eastern Yagikoshu plains, fostering a distinctive regional culture.

The {{nihongo|Ise Coastal Plain|伊勢海岸平野|Ise Kaigan Heiya}} extends along the western seaboard, a narrow strip of lowland between the mountains and the sea that contains the prefectural capital and the majority of the population. The plain is widest near [[Akamatsu]] in the north, where the [[Kurobe River]] delta creates a broad alluvial fan, and narrows progressively southward toward [[Oi]], where the mountains meet the sea in dramatic cliffs.

The [[Echizen Range]] dominates the eastern interior, forming a natural boundary with [[Kochi Prefecture]]. These mountains, rising to over 1,800 meters at [[Mount Echizen]], are heavily forested with hinoki cypress, cedar, and indigenous broadleaf species. The range creates a significant rain shadow effect, with the western slopes receiving heavy precipitation from Sea of Mitoyagi moisture while the eastern leeward valleys remain relatively dry.

The [[Ichijō River]] is the prefecture's primary waterway, originating in the Echizen highlands and flowing westward through the {{nihongo|Ichijō Valley|一乗谷|Ichijō-dani}}—the basin that gives the capital its name—before emptying into the Sea of Mitoyagi near [[Ise-Takahama]]. The river's narrow, steep-sided valley provided the natural defensive position that made Ichijōdani Castle nearly impregnable during the feudal era.

=== Climate ===
Ise Prefecture has a humid subtropical climate (Köppen ''Cfa'') with significant regional variation driven by the Echizen Range's rain shadow. The western coastal zone experiences hot, wet summers and mild, drier winters, moderated by the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]]. The mountain interior is cooler and wetter, with the highest elevations receiving measurable snowfall during the winter months. The prefecture is affected by [[Eastern Typhoons]] that track across the Sea of Mitoyagi during late summer and early autumn, occasionally causing severe flooding in the Ichijō Valley.

