== Economy ==
{{See also|List of companies headquartered in Ise Prefecture}}

Ise Prefecture's economy is driven by three primary sectors: historical and cultural tourism, forestry and timber processing, and mountain agriculture centered on tea cultivation and traditional herbal medicine. The prefecture's GDP in 2025 was approximately 円680 billion, with the service sector—including tourism—accounting for 42% of total output.

=== Tourism and Heritage ===
[[File:Ichijoudani Castle Reconstructed.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The reconstructed [[Ichijōdani Castle]], the centerpiece of the [[Kinzō Incident Heritage Park]] and Ise's most visited historical site.]]

Tourism is the single largest sector of the Ise economy, driven by the prefecture's unparalleled concentration of samurai-era historical sites. The [[Kinzō Incident Heritage Park]], designated a [[National Historical Monument]] in 1954, encompasses the reconstructed [[Ichijōdani Castle]], the {{nihongo|Demon's Gate|鬼門|Kimon}} defile, the [[Ichijō River]] battlefield, and the {{nihongo|Naotaka Memorial Shrine|直隆神社|Naotaka-jinja}}, where the Ii clan leader committed seppuku. The park attracts over 2.4 million visitors annually, making it the third-most-visited historical site in Yagikoshu.

The [[Akamatsu Historical District]], preserving the oldest continuously inhabited settlement in Okaiken, draws an additional 800,000 visitors per year. The district's {{nihongo|Samurai Row|侍通り|Samurai-dōri}}—a preserved street of Ii clan retainers' residences dating to the 1610s—has been used as a filming location for over 40 historical dramas. The [[Ise Heritage Museum]] in central Ichijoudani houses the original {{nihongo|"Honda Letters"|本多書状|Honda-shojō}}, the bloodstained document of forgiveness that Shogun Shigetomo presented to Naotaka as he lay dying, considered one of the most significant artifacts of the Honda Shogunate.

The annual {{nihongo|Kinzō Reconciliation Festival|金造和解祭|Kinzō Wakai-sai}}, held each June 18–20, commemorates the Battle of Ichijoudani with historical reenactments, traditional archery demonstrations, and a solemn lantern ceremony along the Ichijō River. The festival attracts over 350,000 visitors and generates approximately 円12 billion in tourism revenue.

=== Forestry and Timber ===
The [[Echizen Range]] has been the source of Okaiken's finest {{nihongo|hinoki cypress|桧|hinoki}} timber for over a millennium. The {{nihongo|Ise Hinoki|伊勢桧|Ise-hinoki}}, prized for its straight grain, natural rot resistance, and distinctive aromatic scent, commands premium prices in the national construction market. The [[Ise Timber Exchange]], the oldest continuously operating commodity market in western Yagikoshu, sets benchmark prices for hinoki lumber across the island.

Modern forestry in Ise operates under strict sustainability protocols established by the [[Ise Forestry Cooperative]] in 1962. The cooperative manages approximately 180,000 hectares of plantation forest on a 60-year rotation cycle, ensuring that annual harvest volumes never exceed natural growth rates. The [[Echizen Forestry Research Station]], affiliated with the [[Ise Prefectural University]], develops advanced silviculture techniques including disease-resistant hinoki cultivars and precision harvesting methods that minimize soil disturbance on steep slopes.

The timber processing industry is concentrated in the mountain towns of [[Echizen]], [[Minami-Echizen]], and [[Matsuo]], where sawmills and kiln-drying facilities produce dimensional lumber, architectural beams, and specialty products such as {{nihongo|hinoki bath tubs|檜風呂|hinoki-buro}} and {{nihongo|incense cedar|香木|kōboku}}. The [[Ise Woodcraft Association]] maintains quality standards for artisanal products and operates a certification program for traditional woodworking techniques.

=== Agriculture and Tea Cultivation ===
The mountainous terrain of Ise limits large-scale agriculture, but the prefecture's highland microclimates have produced distinctive specialty crops. The {{nihongo|Ise Mountain Tea|伊勢山茶|Ise-yama-cha}}, cultivated on the rain-shadowed eastern slopes of the Echizen Range at elevations between 400 and 800 meters, is one of Okaiken's most prized tea cultivars. The combination of cool mountain air, morning mist, and mineral-rich volcanic soil produces a tea with exceptional {{nihongo|umami|旨味}} depth and a distinctive sweet aftertaste. Approximately 2,800 hectares are dedicated to tea cultivation, producing an estimated 1,200 tonnes of finished tea annually.

The [[Eiheiji Herbal Valley]], centered on the town of [[Eiheiji]], has been the center of traditional Sanese herbal medicine since the Kitayama period. The valley's microclimate supports the cultivation of over 150 medicinal plant species, including {{nihongo|gyokuran|玉蘭}}, {{nihongo|sanshō|山椒}}, and the rare {{nihongo|Ise golden root|伊勢金根|Ise-kinkon}}, a ginseng relative found only in the Echizen highlands. The [[Eiheiji Institute of Traditional Medicine]], established in 1938, maintains the nation's largest herbarium of Sanese medicinal plants and operates a clinical research program integrating traditional herbal remedies with modern pharmacology.

The coastal communities of [[Mihama]], [[Oi]], and [[Ise-Takahama]] support a modest fishing industry, harvesting [[Sea of Mitoyagi]] species including {{nihongo|sawara|鰆}} (Spanish mackerel), {{nihongo|nodoguro|喉黒}} (rosy seabass), and seasonal shellfish. The [[Ise-Takahama Fish Market]] is the primary landing point for the prefecture's fishing fleet, with daily auctions supplying restaurants across western Yagikoshu.

=== Traditional Swordcraft ===
Ise Prefecture is one of the last strongholds of traditional Sanese swordsmithing. The [[Ichijoudani Swordsmith District]], located in the historic {{nihongo|Kaji-machi|鍛冶町}} (Smiths' Quarter) near the castle, has produced blades continuously since the early Honda period. The district's forges, utilizing high-purity {{nihongo|tamahagane|玉鋼}} steel and traditional {{nihongo|tanren|鍛錬}} folding techniques, produce both ceremonial blades and functional kitchen knives that are exported worldwide.

The [[Ise Blade Academy]], founded in 1958, is the nation's only accredited institution for traditional swordsmithing, accepting approximately eight apprentices per year into a rigorous seven-year training program. Graduates receive the {{nihongo|Mukansa|無鑑査}} designation, exempting their work from the standard jury process at national craft exhibitions. The academy's annual exhibition in October draws collectors and martial artists from across Adonia.
Ise's economy gracefully bridges sacred cultural traditions with high-precision optoelectronics. Coastal cooperatives in protected inlets cultivate Akoya pearls through delicate manual nucleus grafting, producing lustrous pearls for international luxury markets. The prefecture preserves ancestral sacred weaving guilds that produce ceremonial unbleached hemp and wild silk vestments for the Ise Grand Shrine, while inland industrial parks manufacture high-purity optical glass, laser prism arrays, and optoelectronic sensors for industrial cameras.

== Transportation ==
Ise Prefecture is connected to the national transportation network primarily through its rail infrastructure and the [[Ise Expressway]], which links the prefectural capital to [[Mito]] in the north and [[Nagayama]] in the south.

=== Rail ===
The [[Ise Main Line]], operated by [[Okaiken Railways]], is the primary rail corridor, connecting [[Ichijoudani Station]] to [[Mito Station]] in approximately 45 minutes on express services. The line continues southward through the Echizen Range via the [[Echizen Tunnel]] to connect with the [[Kochi Main Line]]. The [[Ise Coastal Line]] follows the Sea of Mitoyagi shoreline, serving the fishing communities and port facilities between [[Akamatsu]] and [[Oi]].

The [[Minami Yagikoshu Shinkansen]] passes through Ise Prefecture with a stop at [[Mito Station]] just across the northern border, providing high-speed access to [[Toyozawa]] (approximately 1 hour 15 minutes) and [[Kii]] (approximately 2 hours 30 minutes). A proposed extension of the Shinkansen into central Ise, the {{nihongo|Ise Spur|伊勢枝線|Ise-eshisen}}, has been under discussion since 2015 but remains unfunded due to the prefecture's relatively low population density.

=== Roads ===
The [[Ise Expressway]] (Route 7) is the primary highway, running north-south through the Ichijō Valley and connecting Ichijoudani to Mito and Nagayama. The [[Echizen Mountain Highway]] (Route 302) crosses the Echizen Range via the [[Echizen Pass]] at 1,240 meters, providing access to the eastern Yagikoshu plains. This route is frequently closed during winter due to snowfall and avalanche risk.

The [[Akamatsu Bypass]], completed in 2008 after significant delays caused by the 2004 earthquakes, provides a modern coastal route connecting the northern port cities to the Ichijō Valley without traversing the narrow mountain roads.

=== Air ===
Ise Prefecture is served by the [[Ise-Akamatsu Airport]], a regional facility located 12 kilometers north of the capital near the city of [[Akamatsu]]. The airport operates daily flights to [[Okami]], [[Toyozawa]], and [[Aizawa]], with seasonal service to [[Yomitan]] in [[Yanbaru]]. The airport's single runway was extended in 2018 to accommodate regional jet aircraft, but capacity remains limited.

=== Maritime ===
The [[Akamatsu Port]] is the prefecture's primary maritime facility, handling both commercial cargo and passenger ferry services. Regular ferry routes connect Akamatsu to ports across the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]], including services to [[Mito]] and [[Nagato]]. The port's fish market handles approximately 15,000 tonnes of seafood annually, making it the second-largest landing point on the western Yagikoshu coast after [[Nagato Port]].

== Culture ==
{{Main|Culture of Ise Prefecture}}

=== Cuisine ===
Ise cuisine reflects the prefecture's mountainous geography and coastal access, combining highland agricultural products with fresh Sea of Mitoyagi seafood. The most iconic dish is {{nihongo|Ichijō Nabe|一乗鍋}}, a hearty mountain hot pot traditionally prepared for samurai garrisons during the Honda period. The dish features thick slices of hinoki-smoked pork, mountain vegetables, and Ise Mountain Tea broth, slow-cooked in a heavy iron pot over a wood fire. Modern variations incorporate local herbs from the Eiheiji valley, and the dish is served at over 60 restaurants in the Ichijoudani castle town district.

Other notable dishes include {{nihongo|Akamatsu Sashimi Bowl|赤松刺身丼|Akamatsu-sashimi-don}}, featuring the day's catch from the Sea of Mitoyagi served over Ise Mountain Tea-infused rice; {{nihongo|Echizen Soba|越前蕎麦|Echizen-soba}}, buckwheat noodles made with mountain spring water and served with a distinctive sweet dipping broth; and {{nihongo|Kinzō Mochi|金造餅|Kinzō-mochi}}, a commemorative rice cake filled with sweet red bean paste, shaped to resemble the bloodstained Honda Letters, traditionally consumed during the Kinzō Reconciliation Festival.

=== Festivals ===
The {{nihongo|Kinzō Reconciliation Festival|金造和解祭|Kinzō Wakai-sai}} (June 18–20) is the prefecture's largest cultural event, commemorating the Battle of Ichijoudani with a full-scale historical reenactment involving over 2,000 costumed participants. The festival culminates in the {{nihongo|Lantern of Forgiveness|許しの灯籠|Yurushi no Tōrō}} ceremony, where 10,000 paper lanterns are floated down the Ichijō River at dusk, representing the souls of the fallen from both sides of the conflict.

The {{nihongo|Ise Tea Ceremony|伊勢茶会|Ise-chakai}} (October) is a three-day celebration of the prefecture's tea heritage, featuring competitions among local cultivators, public tastings, and demonstrations of traditional tea-processing methods. The {{nihongo|Akamatsu Maritime Festival|赤松海祭|Akamatsu Umi-sai}} (August) honors the Sea of Mitoyagi with dragon boat races, fishing competitions, and a fireworks display over the harbor.

=== Arts and Crafts ===
Beyond swordsmithing, Ise Prefecture maintains several traditional craft traditions. The {{nihongo|Ise Lacquerware|伊勢漆器|Ise-shikki}} tradition, centered on the town of [[Ikeda]], produces distinctive red-and-black lacquered vessels using techniques refined during the Honda period. The [[Ise Pottery]] tradition of [[Takanashi]], utilizing local Echizen clay, produces functional stoneware with a characteristic ash glaze that has been favored by tea ceremony practitioners for centuries.

The {{nihongo|Ichijō Textile|一乗織|Ichijō-ori}} is a rare silk weaving technique developed by the Ii clan's retainers, featuring intricate geometric patterns that encode clan genealogies and battle histories. The technique nearly died out in the 1960s but was revived through a prefectural preservation program, and today approximately 15 registered practitioners produce limited quantities for ceremonial use and museum collections.

== Education ==
Ise Prefecture is home to several institutions of higher education. The [[Ise Prefectural University]], located in Ichijoudani, is the primary institution, offering programs in history, forestry, and traditional arts alongside standard academic curricula. Its [[Department of Samurai Studies]] is considered the nation's leading center for Honda Shogunate historiography, and its faculty has produced the definitive academic editions of the Kinzō Incident archives.

The [[Eiheiji Institute of Traditional Medicine]] in [[Eiheiji]] trains practitioners in Sanese herbal medicine and operates a clinical research program. The [[Ise Blade Academy]] in the Ichijoudani Kaji-machi district provides the nation's only accredited swordsmithing apprenticeship. The [[Akamatsu Maritime College]] in [[Akamatsu]] offers programs in marine science, navigation, and fisheries management.

== Demographics ==
Ise Prefecture has a population of 1,770,114 (2025), distributed across 17 municipalities. The population has declined gradually since the 2004 earthquakes, which accelerated an existing outmigration trend as younger residents relocated to the larger metropolitan centers of [[Mito]] and [[Toyozawa]]. The prefecture has an aging population profile, with over 28% of residents aged 65 or above, significantly higher than the national average.

